A practical, Azure-aligned naming convention you can adopt as-is:

## Pattern
`<resource-prefix>-<project/app>-<env>-<region>[-<instance>]`

## Resource prefixes (Microsoft's recommended abbreviations)
| Resource | Prefix |
|---|---|
| Resource Group | `rg` |
| Service Bus Namespace | `sb` |
| Web PubSub | `wps` |
| Queue | (no prefix — use descriptive name) |
| Topic | (no prefix — use descriptive name) |

## Environment codes
`dev`, `test`, `uat`, `prod`

## Region codes (shorten to keep names compact)
`eus` (East US), `wus` (West US), `weu` (West Europe), etc.

## Examples by resource type

**Resource Group:**
`rg-orders-dev-eus`

**Service Bus Namespace:**
`sb-orders-dev-eus` (must be globally unique — append a short suffix if needed, e.g. `sb-orders-dev-eus-01`)

**Web PubSub:**
`wps-orders-dev-eus`

**Queue** — pattern: `<domain>-<entity>-<action>-q`
- `orders-payment-failed-q`
- `inventory-stock-updated-q`

**Topic** — pattern: `<domain>-<entity>-<event>-t`
- `orders-created-t`
- `shipment-status-changed-t`

**Subscription** — pattern: `<topic-name>-<consumer/purpose>-sub`
- `orders-created-t-billing-sub`
- `orders-created-t-notifications-sub`

## Rules to keep consistent
- All lowercase, hyphen-separated (no spaces, no underscores, no camelCase)
- Keep names descriptive but short — Service Bus names max 50 chars (namespace), 260 chars (queue/topic), but readability matters more than the limit
- No environment code in queue/topic/subscription names — environment is already scoped by which namespace they live in
- Be consistent on domain/entity terms across the team (agree on a short glossary, e.g. "orders" not "order" vs "Orders")

